  "original_html": "<h1 id=\"primary-heading\" class=\"sr-only\">Home</h1><div class=\"flex flex-col gap-8\"><div class=\"bg-white p-4 shadow rounded-lg sticky top-8\"><div class=\"flex justify-between items-center\"><div class=\"flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-center gap-4\"><div class=\"w-12 h-12\"><img class=\"object-cover rounded-md w-full h-full\" src=\"/img/k/public/a11cab2-Module%202%20Reflective%20Cultivating%20Literacy.png\" alt=\"Badge or credential logo image\"/></div><div class=\"flex flex-col\"><div><b>Name:</b> <a href=\"/u/stacey-ellison\" class=\"underline text-blue-600\">Stacey Ellison</a></div><div><b>Badge:</b> <a href=\"/badge/913634e9-4cfb-4cfc-8c54-c2cebc301d7e\" class=\"underline text-blue-600\">Reading to Write: They Say / I Say</a></div></div></div><div></div></div></div><section title=\"Request responses\" class=\"w-full space-y-5 bg-white p-4\"><div class=\"flex flex-col gap-2\"><div class=\"font-bold\">Which version of the They Say / I Say routine (with visual texts or with annotation) feels like the best fit for your students right now? Why?:</div><div class=\"flex flex-col gap-2\"><p>Annotation is the best fit, it is already part of my classroom teaching.</p></div></div><div class=\"flex flex-col gap-2\"><div class=\"font-bold\">Describe one way you might adapt the They Say / I Say framework to align with the types of texts your students read most often (e.g., articles, visuals, multimodal texts).:</div><div class=\"flex flex-col gap-2\"><p>We read classic short stories, I may have students think of the conversation as interacting with the characters...breaking the 4th wall.</p></div></div><div class=\"flex flex-col gap-2\"><div class=\"font-bold\">What, if anything, do you need help thinking through as you plan for using They Say / I Say in your classroom?:</div><div class=\"flex flex-col gap-2\"><p>Nothing, I am good. Excited to use this one!</p></div></div></section></div>",
